<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/05AG">Remark <span data-tag="05AG">4.8.2.21</span>.</a> In the situation of Proposition <a data-tag="05AF" href="/tag/05AF">4.8.2.20</a>, suppose that $f$ is a Kan fibration. Then assertions $(a)$ and $(b)$ can be reformulated as follows: </p>
<p><dl>
<dt>$(a')$</dt>
<dd><p>
The Kan fibration $f$ is $0$-full if and only if, for each vertex $y \in Y$, the fiber $X_{y} = \{ y\}  \times _{Y} X$ is nonempty. 
</p>
</dd>
<dt>$(b')$</dt>
<dd><p>
For $n \geq 1$, the Kan fibration $f$ is $n$-full if and only if, for every vertex $x \in X$ having image $y = f(x)$, the set $\pi _{n-1}( X_{y}, x)$ consists of a single element. 
</p>
</dd>
</dl></p>
<p> See Corollary <a data-tag="050Z" href="/tag/050Z">3.2.6.8</a> (and Variant <a data-tag="0510" href="/tag/0510">3.2.6.9</a> for the case $n = 1$). </p>
</article>
